## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8 Cooperative Cancellation

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code uses `Thread.Abort()` to kill a thread exceeding a time limit. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it simply doesn't work.

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Block until completion or timeout
            if (task.Wait(timeout))
                return task.Result;

            cts.Cancel();
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerException is not null)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException);
        }
    }
}
```

### If `work` is long-running and must respond to cancellation:

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        try
        {
            var task = Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token);
            return task.GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

Callers pass a cancellation-aware delegate:

```csharp
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
{
    foreach (var item in items)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // cooperative checkpoint
        Process(item);
    }
    return computedResult;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

---

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| **Cancellation isn't immediate** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, cooperative cancellation only works at checkpoints. If `work` is a single blocking call (e.g., `Thread.Sleep(60000)`), cancellation won't interrupt it. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers. Always wrap in `using`. |
| **Forgetting to pass token through the call chain** | Every layer must forward the token or cancellation stalls. |
| **Uncooperative code (can't modify `work`)** | If the delegate can't check a token (e.g., third-party library), move it to a **child process** and use `Process.Kill()` on timeout. There is no in-process equivalent to `Thread.Abort` in modern .NET. |
| **`Thread.ResetAbort` → no equivalent needed** | The catch block with `ResetAbort` was preventing thread teardown. With cooperative cancellation, you simply check `token.IsCancellationRequested` and decide to continue. |

---

### What Was Removed

| Old API | Replacement |
|---------|-------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Removed entirely — not needed |
| `new Thread(...)` + `thread.Start()` | `Task.Run(...)` (uses thread pool) |

---

### Verification Checklist

- ✅ No `Thread.Abort`, `ThreadAbortException`, or `ResetAbort` references remain
- ✅ Builds cleanly on `net8.0` with no `SYSLIB0006` warnings
- ✅ `CancellationTokenSource` is disposed via `using`
- ✅ Timeout still throws `TimeoutException` for caller compatibility
